{% set local_problem %}
<p>Many Madison homes have a downspout problem they don't even know about. Builder-grade downspouts are often the minimum size code allows — fine for light rain, but overwhelmed during the heavy storms North Alabama experiences regularly.</p>

<p>When downspouts can't keep up, water backs up in your gutters and overflows. Even worse, many downspouts simply dump water right next to the foundation. On Madison's clay soil, that water doesn't absorb — it pools, and eventually finds its way into your basement or crawlspace.</p>

<p>We see this constantly in Madison subdivisions: homes with technically "working" gutter systems that are actually funneling thousands of gallons of water directly toward the foundation every year. The fix is usually straightforward — properly sized downspouts positioned and extended to move water away from your home.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has three components: adequate capacity, proper positioning, and effective extensions.</p>

<p>Capacity means having enough downspouts, sized appropriately for your roof area and local rainfall. Madison's 55+ inches of annual rain means we often recommend 3x4 inch downspouts instead of standard 2x3 inch — they handle heavy storms without backing up.</p>

<p>Positioning means placing downspouts where water naturally flows to corners and low points in your gutter system. Extensions (or better yet, underground drains) then carry that water well away from your foundation — not just a few inches past your landscaping.</p>
{% endset %}

